# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which
# rewrites checked-out files to CRLF. For most files that is harmless. For a
# shell script it is not: a `#!/bin/sh` line stored or checked out with CRLF
# becomes `#!/bin/sh\r`, and the kernel then looks for an interpreter literally
# named "sh\r", failing with the notoriously unhelpful
#
#     bash: ./install.sh: /bin/sh^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# Pinning eol=lf makes the working copy LF regardless of core.autocrlf, so the
# file works when cloned as well as when piped from raw.githubusercontent.com.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell is fine with either ending, but pinning CRLF keeps Windows editors
# (including Notepad, which some users will open this in) from showing it as a
# single run-on line.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# GitHub Actions runs these on Linux runners; heredocs and multi-line `run:`
# blocks are executed by bash there, so CRLF would leak \r into shell variables.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Rust, TypeScript, and web sources: normalise to LF in the repository so
# diffs stay clean across platforms.
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.jsx text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf

# Never touch binary content.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.wasm binary
